{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 18, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and a prince of Galilee and Tiberias from 1174 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was born in 1140 in Tripoli Lebanon. He became the count of Tripoli in 1152 after the death of his father.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was raised in the court of his mother's brother King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. He was educated in the Latin Church and received military training from the Knights Templar. In 1152 Raymond III succeeded his father as count of Tripoli. He was only 12 years old at the time and the county was governed by his mother until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures in 1174 and became the prince of Galilee and Tiberias. He played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a close advisor to King Amalric I. Raymond III also fought in several battles against the Muslim forces of Nur ad-Din and Saladin. In 1174 he led a successful campaign against the city of Homs and in 1177 he fought in the Battle of Montgisard.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin*\nIn 1187 Raymond III led the army of the Kingdom of Jerusalem in the Battle of Hattin. The battle was a disaster for the Christians and Raymond III was forced to flee the battlefield. The city of Tiberias was captured by Saladin's forces and Raymond III's wife Eschiva was taken prisoner. Raymond III died in 1187 possibly in the city of Tripoli.\n\n*Legacy*\nRaymond III was a prominent figure in the history of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli. He played a key role in the politics and military campaigns of the kingdom and was a close advisor to several kings. Despite his defeat at the Battle of Hattin Raymond III is remembered as a brave and chivalrous knight.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.00426335